--- /dev/null
+++ b/evidence/run_logged.sh
@@ -0,0 +1,26 @@
+#!/usr/bin/env bash
+set -u
+
+if [[ "$#" -lt 2 ]]; then
+  printf 'usage: %s LOG_NAME COMMAND [ARG ...]\n' "$0" >&2
+  exit 64
+fi
+
+log_name="$1"
+shift
+log_path="/audit-output/evidence/${log_name}"
+
+{
+  printf 'COMMAND:'
+  printf ' %q' "$@"
+  printf '\n'
+} > "$log_path"
+
+set +e
+"$@" >> "$log_path" 2>&1
+status=$?
+set -e
+
+printf '\nEXIT_STATUS: %d\n' "$status" >> "$log_path"
+cat "$log_path"
+exit "$status"

codex
The fixed-versus-extended witnesses are decisive: the installed loop rule proves that `word` remains empty after iterating over `"ba"` (`#Top`), while the same claim under the pre-summary theory is rejected with the real residual `"ba"`. Separate witnesses also show the split bridge suppresses its required allocation and the word-sort bridge suppresses two required allocations. I’m completing the independent false-result mutation and trust accounting next.
